# D&D AI — Environment Variables (example)
#
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and adjust. Per docs/PLAN.md §24.0 and ADR 0012,
# self-hosted Docker Compose is the officially supported deployment topology
# — DATABASE_URL points at a LOCAL/SELF-HOSTED PostgreSQL 18 server by
# default. See docs/DEVELOPMENT.md §3 for install and setup, and §3.6 for the
# `compose.yaml` workflow. CI verifies the same work against a disposable
# containerized PostgreSQL 18 instance; AWS dev RDS is an optional, no
# longer required path (docs/DEVELOPMENT.md §3.5).
#
# NEVER put a real credential in this file or in .env. For self-hosted use,
# these variables (and compose.yaml's environment interpolation) ARE the
# runtime secret mechanism — compose.yaml requires POSTGRES_PASSWORD to be
# set to something and refuses to start otherwise (see below); nothing in
# this repository ships a working default password. For the optional AWS
# path, runtime secrets come from AWS Secrets Manager or IAM database
# authentication instead — see CLAUDE.md rule 10 and
# docs/DATABASE_CONVENTIONS.md §27.3. .env itself is gitignored, but treat
# any password you paste into DATABASE_URL as sensitive regardless.

# =============================================================================
# Application
# =============================================================================

DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=local
DND_AI_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Where to read one-file-per-setting host-mounted secrets from (the
# conventional Docker/Compose secrets mount) — optional locally, since a
# nonexistent directory is simply skipped. Always a real environment
# variable, never something this file itself supplies in production (see
# above) — it names *where other secrets live*.
# DND_AI_SECRETS_DIR=/run/secrets

# =============================================================================
# Database — local/self-hosted PostgreSQL 18 (default)
# =============================================================================
# The major version MUST match what CI runs (docs/DATABASE_CONVENTIONS.md
# §2.1). A server on a different major version produces green local runs
# that fail CI — that is a setup defect, not a preference.
#
# Fastest setup: `docker compose up -d db` — see docs/DEVELOPMENT.md §3.6.
# Without Docker: install PostgreSQL 18 natively, then
# `createdb -U postgres dnd_ai`. Either way the six project roles are
# created by the 001_bootstrap migration, not by hand.
#
# REQUIRED for `docker compose up`: compose.yaml has NO fallback default
# for POSTGRES_PASSWORD — it refuses to start without one, on purpose (no
# repo-committed password, ever, including for local development).

# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=set-a-real-value-here

# REQUIRED for `docker compose --profile tools run --rm migrate`: the
# complete SQLAlchemy URL the migrate service connects with. Compose does
# NOT build this for you from POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_PASSWORD/POSTGRES_DB
# above — that would be plain string interpolation, not a URL encoder, so
# it would silently break for a password containing characters that are
# special in a URL (@ : / ? # [ ] % and similar). Build it yourself,
# percent-encoding the password segment if it needs one
# (Python: `urllib.parse.quote(password, safe="")`), and keep it in sync
# with POSTGRES_PASSWORD above by hand — nothing does that automatically.
# Note the host is `db` (the compose service name, reached over the
# compose-internal network), not `localhost`.
#
# Example, for a password containing "@" (percent-encoded below as %40):
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD=p@ssw0rd
#   MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres:p%40ssw0rd@db:5432/dnd_ai
# (illustrative only — never reuse an example password for anything real)

# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres:set-a-real-value-here@db:5432/dnd_ai

# REQUIRED for `docker compose up -d api` (or plain `docker compose up`,
# which brings up every non-tools-profile service): the complete
# SQLAlchemy URL the `api` service's DND_AI_DATABASE_URL reads. Same host
# (`db`, not `localhost`) as MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL above, but a
# deliberately different IDENTITY, not merely a different variable name:
# this URL MUST authenticate as **app_read_write** — the DML-only login
# role 001_bootstrap creates (SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on application
# tables; no schema DDL, no role management, no membership in
# migration_owner, not a superuser, not a schema owner, no CREATEDB/
# CREATEROLE/BYPASSRLS) — and NEVER `postgres`, `migration_runner`, or
# `migration_owner`. Running the API under any of those three would give
# every authenticated request the ability to alter schema, manage roles,
# or bypass row-level security; app_read_write cannot do any of that even
# if application code has a bug, which is the entire point of a separate,
# constrained credential for this service (docs/DATABASE_CONVENTIONS.md
# §27.1, ADR 0009).
#
# app_read_write is created by 001_bootstrap (part of `migrate`) with NO
# password — password auth is refused until one is set. Provision it with:
#
#   uv run python scripts/operations/database_recovery.py set-role-password \
#     --role app_read_write --password-env-var APP_READ_WRITE_PASSWORD \
#     --project dnd_ai --env-file .env --compose-file compose.yaml
#
# (APP_READ_WRITE_PASSWORD is read from THIS process's own shell
# environment by that command — it is not a Settings field, not read by
# Compose or the application, and is never accepted as a `--password`
# flag, which would leave it visible in `ps`/shell history for as long as
# the command runs; `--password-file` is the equivalent for a mounted
# secret instead of an environment variable.) Then build this URL
# yourself from the same password, percent-encoded the same way
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL is above, and keep the three in sync by hand —
# nothing derives one from another. Safe to rerun any time to rotate the
# password; migration and application credentials remain independently
# rotatable since they are always two different roles. Run `uv run python
# scripts/operations/database_recovery.py verify-roles --project dnd_ai
# --env-file .env --compose-file compose.yaml` afterward to confirm the
# password was actually set before starting `api` — see
# docs/DEVELOPMENT.md §3.6 for the full required sequence and why the
# ordering matters.
#
# `docker compose up -d api` ALSO requires the three API_OIDC_* variables
# in the "OIDC login provider" section further down this file —
# compose.yaml runs the `api` service with DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production
# unconditionally (this file is the self-hosted/production deployment
# topology, not a "local" convenience default), so a real, HTTPS-only OIDC
# provider is always required to start it, not merely optional the way it
# is for a bare `uv run uvicorn ...` invocation outside Docker.

# APP_READ_WRITE_PASSWORD=set-a-real-value-here
# API_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://app_read_write:set-a-real-value-here@db:5432/dnd_ai

# DATABASE_URL below is what the application/tests use from the host — a
# third, independent setting. It addresses `localhost` (only reachable via
# compose.override.yaml's port mapping, i.e. plain `docker compose up -d db`
# with no `-f` flags — see docs/DEVELOPMENT.md §3.6) and must be kept in
# sync with POSTGRES_PASSWORD by hand, the same way MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL
# is. It has the same percent-encoding requirement as MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL
# above if the password contains a URL-special character.
#
# This is also the value Alembic, pytest, and CI read directly, and the one
# the application accepts as a local/test-only compatibility alias (see the
# top of this file) — production never loads this file at all, so this
# value can never reach a production process by accident; production sets
# DND_AI_DATABASE_URL as a real environment variable or mounted secret
# instead.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres:set-a-real-value-here@localhost:5432/dnd_ai

# Separate database for manual local runs. tests/conftest.py provisions its own
# ephemeral database per session regardless, so this isn't what the test suite
# uses — it's for poking around with psql.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres:set-a-real-value-here@localhost:5432/dnd_ai_test

# Rest of compose.yaml's service configuration — only read by `docker
# compose`, not by the application directly. Change these together with
# DATABASE_URL/MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL above if you use non-default values.
# POSTGRES_USER=postgres
# POSTGRES_DB=dnd_ai
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# API_PORT=8000

# =============================================================================
# Database — AWS dev (optional, no longer required)
# =============================================================================
# Only relevant if you have deployed the optional Terraform under terraform/
# and want to reproduce a failure against it or inspect it directly. Open
# network access first with `scripts/aws-db-allow-my-ip.sh open`, and close it
# when you're done — nothing does this automatically.
#
# Build this from Terraform outputs plus the master secret's password:
#   terraform -chdir=terraform/environments/dev output database_endpoint
#   aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
#     --secret-id $(terraform -chdir=terraform/environments/dev output -raw database_secret_name) \
#     --query SecretString --output text
#
# `sslmode=require` is not optional against dev — a stock local/self-hosted
# server has no TLS configured, whereas dev's parameter group rejects a
# plain connection outright.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://dnd_admin:<password>@<endpoint>:5432/dnd_ai?sslmode=require

# =============================================================================
# AWS (optional)
# =============================================================================
# Not required for schema or application work, or for CI (docs/CONTRIBUTING.md §2).
# Only needed if you use the optional AWS RDS path.

AWS_REGION=us-east-1
AWS_PROFILE=dnd-ai-dev

# =============================================================================
# Secrets Manager references (optional AWS path)
# =============================================================================
# Names only — the application resolves values at runtime. Confirm the actual
# names with: terraform -chdir=terraform/environments/dev output

# SECRET_OPENAI=dnd-ai/dev/openai/api-key
# SECRET_DISCORD=dnd-ai/dev/discord/bot-token

# =============================================================================
# Feature flags
# =============================================================================

DND_AI_FEATURE_AI_NPC_DIALOGUE=false
DND_AI_FEATURE_DISCORD_INTEGRATION=false
DND_AI_FEATURE_FOUNDRY_INTEGRATION=false

# =============================================================================
# AI provider (src/dnd_ai/domain/ai_provider.py — OpenAiCompatibleProvider)
# =============================================================================
# Disabled by default (DND_AI_FEATURE_AI_NPC_DIALOGUE=false above). The
# credential this integration wants is an OpenAI **Platform** API key
# (platform.openai.com/api-keys, billed per API call) — NOT a ChatGPT Plus/
# Pro subscription key (there is no such thing usable here) and NOT an
# Anthropic API key; those are different products with different keys.
#
# Two supported targets, both served by the same provider class:
#
# 1. Hosted OpenAI (default, no API_AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL needed): requires a
#    real API key once you turn DND_AI_FEATURE_AI_NPC_DIALOGUE on for a
#    `docker compose up -d api` deployment — compose.yaml runs `api` with
#    DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production unconditionally, and
#    dnd_ai.config.Settings refuses to start in that combination with no key
#    configured (env var or mounted secret).
# 2. An operator-supplied, locally hosted, OpenAI-API-compatible model
#    server (Ollama, vLLM, LM Studio, text-generation-webui, llama.cpp's own
#    server, ...): set API_AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL to that server's own
#    `.../v1` base URL. A key is then optional — that server's own access
#    control is the operator's concern, not this application's.
#
# Prefer a mounted secret over API_AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY for a real hosted key:
# mount it at ${DND_AI_SECRETS_DIR}/dnd_ai_ai_provider_api_key inside the
# `api` container (the same host-mounted-secret-file mechanism
# DND_AI_DATABASE_URL's own secret file already uses; see
# src/dnd_ai/config.py's module docstring) instead of setting
# API_AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY here — an environment variable set in .env/
# compose.yaml is still visible via `docker inspect`/`docker compose
# config`, which a mounted file is not. Never put a real key directly in
# this file, in compose.yaml, in source control, or in any rendered
# documentation — DND_AI_AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY is also never logged or printed
# by the application.
#
# All four map through compose.yaml to the container's real DND_AI_* setting
# (the same API_-prefixed/DND_AI_-prefixed host-side/container-side split as
# API_DATABASE_URL/DND_AI_DATABASE_URL and API_OIDC_*/DND_AI_OIDC_* above),
# each with a safe soft default via compose.yaml's own `${VAR:-default}`
# interpolation — left unset, the feature stays off and no key is sent.

# API_FEATURE_AI_NPC_DIALOGUE=false
# API_AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY=set-a-real-openai-platform-api-key-here
# API_AI_PROVIDER_MODEL=gpt-4o
# API_AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1

# =============================================================================
# OIDC login provider (src/dnd_ai/api/auth.py, src/dnd_ai/domain/tokens.py)
# =============================================================================
# Two independent ways these reach dnd_ai.config.Settings, with different
# requirements:
#
# 1. Running the API directly on the host (`uv run uvicorn dnd_ai.api.app:app
#    ...`, DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT left at its "local" default): optional — the
#    API skeleton and its non-authenticated endpoints (/healthz, /readyz)
#    run without any of the three DND_AI_OIDC_* variables below. Set them
#    directly (not the API_OIDC_* names below — those are Compose-only,
#    see point 2) if you want to exercise an authenticated route locally.
#
# 2. `docker compose up -d api`: REQUIRED, with no fallback default,
#    exactly like API_DATABASE_URL above. compose.yaml runs `api` with
#    DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production unconditionally (a fixed value in
#    compose.yaml itself, not sourced from this file) — this file is the
#    self-hosted/production deployment topology (ADR 0012), not a "local"
#    convenience default, so it must never be possible to start it with no
#    OIDC provider configured at all and have every authenticated route
#    silently fail closed with a generic 500 instead of the intended 401.
#    Set API_OIDC_ISSUER/API_OIDC_AUDIENCE/API_OIDC_JWKS_URL below —
#    compose.yaml maps each to the container's real DND_AI_OIDC_* setting;
#    the API_-prefixed names exist only on the host side, in this file,
#    the same distinction API_DATABASE_URL/DND_AI_DATABASE_URL already
#    makes.
#
# In both cases, all three settings are required together — a partial
# configuration (some but not all three) is rejected — and, because
# DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production is what compose.yaml always sets, the
# issuer and JWKS URL Compose starts the container with MUST be absolute,
# credential-free, fragment-free HTTPS URLs with a host (a plain-HTTP JWKS
# endpoint would let a network-positioned attacker substitute the key set),
# and the audience must be non-empty with no leading/trailing whitespace —
# dnd_ai.config.Settings rejects anything else outright at process startup,
# before /readyz can report success or an authenticated route is ever
# called. Running the API directly on the host with DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT
# still at "local" additionally accepts plain HTTP (for a private/
# in-process test identity provider), but the same all-or-nothing and
# structural rules otherwise still apply. No self-hosted OIDC provider is
# wired into compose.yaml itself; point these at whatever standards-
# compliant OIDC provider you actually run.

# DND_AI_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-idp.example/realms/dnd-ai
# DND_AI_OIDC_AUDIENCE=dnd-ai-api
# DND_AI_OIDC_JWKS_URL=https://your-idp.example/realms/dnd-ai/protocol/openid-connect/certs

# REQUIRED for `docker compose up -d api` — see point 2 above. Must be the
# same real OIDC provider's issuer/audience/JWKS URL as the direct
# DND_AI_OIDC_* variables above; kept as separate variables only because
# Compose's own interpolation needs distinct host-side names from the
# container-side settings it maps them to (the same reason API_DATABASE_URL
# is distinct from DND_AI_DATABASE_URL).

# API_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-idp.example/realms/dnd-ai
# API_OIDC_AUDIENCE=dnd-ai-api
# API_OIDC_JWKS_URL=https://your-idp.example/realms/dnd-ai/protocol/openid-connect/certs

# =============================================================================
# Foundry CORS (src/dnd_ai/config.py, src/dnd_ai/api/app.py)
# =============================================================================
# The FoundryVTT module runs as browser JavaScript on its own origin,
# separate from this API's in the documented deployment topology
# (docs/LOCAL_DEPLOYMENT.md — e.g. https://foundry.example.com talking to
# https://world.example.com/api). A real browser therefore sends a CORS
# preflight before every authenticated request; with no allowlist
# configured, those preflights fail and the module cannot reach the API at
# all — not a silent security hole, a loud functional one, which is the
# safe direction for an unconfigured value to fail in.
#
# DND_AI_FOUNDRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS is a comma-separated list of EXACT origins
# (scheme://host[:port] — never a path, query, fragment, embedded
# credential, or wildcard/wildcarded-subdomain pattern; dnd_ai.config.
# Settings rejects anything else outright at process startup). Optional —
# leaving it unset (the default) simply means no browser origin can reach
# this API cross-origin; it never falls back to permitting one. Required,
# with no fallback, once DND_AI_FEATURE_FOUNDRY_INTEGRATION=true AND
# DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production are both true. compose.yaml always runs
# `api` with DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production, so once you turn the Foundry
# integration on for a self-hosted deployment, dnd_ai.config.Settings
# additionally requires every configured origin to be HTTPS (running the
# API directly on the host with DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT left at "local" also
# accepts plain HTTP, mirroring the DND_AI_OIDC_* settings' identical
# local/production split above).

# DND_AI_FEATURE_FOUNDRY_INTEGRATION=false
# DND_AI_FOUNDRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://foundry.example.com

# REQUIRED together for `docker compose up -d api` only if you want the
# FoundryVTT module to reach this deployment — see the section above for
# the underlying settings. Same host-side/container-side naming split as
# API_OIDC_*/DND_AI_OIDC_* — compose.yaml maps each to the container's real
# DND_AI_* setting. Left unset, both map to a safe default (feature flag
# off, empty allowlist) via compose.yaml's own `${VAR:-default}`
# interpolation, not a hard requirement like API_OIDC_* above.

# API_FEATURE_FOUNDRY_INTEGRATION=false
# API_FOUNDRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://foundry.example.com
